      "format": "essay",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "context": "Mei, a student in Little Rock, studies catalase, an enzyme in liver tissue that breaks down hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen gas. She measures the rate of oxygen production in mL per minute. In each trial she uses the same mass of liver homogenate and the same volume and concentration of hydrogen peroxide.\n\n**Experiment 1: Temperature** (pH 7, 2% hydrogen peroxide)\n\n| Temperature (°C) | Mean rate of O2 production (mL/min) |\n| ---: | ---: |\n| 5 | 1.2 |\n| 15 | 2.8 |\n| 25 | 4.6 |\n| 37 | 7.5 |\n| 50 | 3.1 |\n| 70 | 0.2 |\n\n**Experiment 2: pH** (37 °C, 2% hydrogen peroxide)\n\n| pH | Mean rate of O2 production (mL/min) |\n| ---: | ---: |\n| 3 | 0.8 |\n| 5 | 3.9 |\n| 7 | 7.4 |\n| 9 | 4.2 |\n| 11 | 0.9 |\n\n**Experiment 3: Inhibitor** (37 °C, pH 7). Mei repeats the reaction at several hydrogen peroxide concentrations, with and without a molecule called inhibitor X. Inhibitor X has a shape similar to hydrogen peroxide.\n\n| Hydrogen peroxide (%) | Rate without inhibitor X (mL/min) | Rate with inhibitor X (mL/min) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| 0.5 | 2.1 | 1.0 |\n| 1 | 3.9 | 1.9 |\n| 2 | 6.0 | 3.1 |\n| 4 | 7.4 | 5.0 |\n| 8 | 7.6 | 7.3 |",
      "question": "Use the data from Experiments 1, 2 and 3 to answer the following.\n\n(a) Identify the independent variable in Experiment 1. Describe the effect of increasing temperature on the rate of catalase activity, including the temperature at which the enzyme is most active in this experiment.\n\n(b) Explain, in terms of enzyme structure, why the rate at 70 °C is much lower than the rate at 37 °C.\n\n(c) Explain how the data from Experiment 2 show that catalase activity depends on pH, and explain how a pH far from the optimum changes the enzyme at the molecular level.\n\n(d) Identify the type of inhibition that inhibitor X most likely causes. Justify your claim using the data from Experiment 3.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "criterion": "(a) Identify and describe",
          "description": "Identifies temperature as the independent variable (1 point) AND describes that the rate increases with temperature up to an optimum of about 37 °C (7.5 mL/min) and then decreases at higher temperatures (1 point). The 1 point for the independent variable is a separate requirement from the 1 point for the description.",
          "points": 2
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(b) Explain effect of high temperature",
          "description": "Explains that high temperature disrupts the bonds (such as hydrogen and ionic bonds) that maintain the enzyme's tertiary structure, so the protein denatures (1 point), changing the shape of the active site so the substrate can no longer bind, which lowers the rate (1 point).",
          "points": 2
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(c) Explain pH effect with data",
          "description": "Uses Experiment 2 data to show the rate is highest near pH 7 and falls at both lower and higher pH (1 point). Explains that extreme pH changes the ionization or charge of amino acid R groups, disrupting the ionic and hydrogen bonds that hold the enzyme's shape and altering the active site, which reduces substrate binding (1 point).",
          "points": 2
        },
        {
          "criterion": "(d) Identify and justify inhibition type",
          "description": "Identifies competitive inhibition (1 point). Justifies it with data: the inhibitor lowers the rate at low substrate concentration (such as 1.0 vs. 2.1 mL/min at 0.5%), but at high substrate concentration the rate nearly matches the uninhibited rate (7.3 vs. 7.6 mL/min at 8%). This is because the inhibitor, which resembles the substrate, competes for the active site and excess substrate outcompetes it (1 point).",
          "points": 2
        }
      ],
      "keyPoints": [
        "Independent variable is temperature; the rate rises to an optimum near 37 °C and then drops sharply.",
        "High temperature denatures the enzyme by breaking the bonds that maintain its tertiary structure, changing the active site shape.",
        "Experiment 2 shows an optimum near pH 7; extreme pH alters the charges of R groups, disrupting bonds and the active site.",
        "Inhibitor X is a competitive inhibitor because it resembles the substrate and binds the active site.",
        "The data support competitive inhibition because high substrate concentration nearly overcomes the inhibitor, so the rates are almost equal at 8%."
      ],
      "sampleOutline": "(a) The independent variable is temperature. The rate rises from 1.2 mL/min at 5 °C to a maximum of 7.5 mL/min at 37 °C, then falls to 3.1 at 50 °C and 0.2 at 70 °C. Collisions and kinetic energy increase up to the optimum, and past it the enzyme is damaged. (b) At 70 °C, heat breaks the hydrogen and ionic bonds that hold the tertiary structure. The enzyme denatures, the active site changes shape, hydrogen peroxide no longer fits, and few products form. (c) The rate peaks at pH 7 (7.4 mL/min) and falls at pH 3 (0.8) and pH 11 (0.9). Extreme pH changes the ionization of R groups, disrupting ionic and hydrogen bonds. The active site shape changes and substrate binding drops. (d) Inhibitor X is a competitive inhibitor. It resembles the substrate and competes for the active site. At 0.5% peroxide it cuts the rate from 2.1 to 1.0, but at 8% the rates are nearly equal (7.6 vs. 7.3) because the large amount of substrate outcompetes the inhibitor."
    }
  ]
}

